394 0 obj
<< /AP << /N 1085 0 R >> /C [ 1.0 0.819611 0.0 ] /CA 0.399994 /Contents (There are numerous studies on manuscript spellings and the phonological systems behind these spellings. This has been one of the major tasks of Slavic philology since the 19th century. In fact, I would argue that it is the best studied aspect of OCS. Previous literature should be discussed here.) /CreationDate (D:20260826173223+02'00') /F 4 /M (D:20260914175622+02'00') /NM (e32ace31-7cef-49f4-b614-dce1acf9e49a) /P 35 0 R /Popup 1086 0 R /QuadPoints [ 252.267 730.573 516.22 730.573 252.267 715.153 516.22 715.153 56.8 716.773 528.301 716.773 56.8 701.353 528.301 701.353 56.8 702.973 90.3634 702.973 56.8 687.553 90.3634 687.553 ] /RC (<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:26.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2" ><p dir="ltr"><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.6pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal">There are numerous studies on manuscript spellings and the phonological systems behind these spellings. This has been one of the major tasks of Slavic philology since the 19th century. In fact, I would argue that it is the best studied aspect of OCS. Previous literature should be discussed here.</span></p></body>) /Rect [ 56.3181 687.071 528.782 731.055 ] /Subj (Hervorheben) /Subtype /Highlight /T (Anonymous) /Type /Annot >>
endobj
