418 0 obj
<< /AP << /N 1121 0 R >> /C [ 1.0 0.819611 0.0 ] /CA 0.399994 /Contents (Here the issues start. There is no consensus about the relative chronology of these two palatalizations. In fact, the most elaborate reconstructions of the relative chronology of Slavic sound changes date the Progressive Palatalization \(= Third Palatalization\) before the monophthongization of diphthongs, e.g. Holzer \(2007\) \(= Pedersen's chronology\). The arguments for doing so have most recently been discussed by Wandl \(2020\). I also doubt that many scholars would call a phoneme system prior to these palatalization "Late Common Slavic". Unfortunately, the authors do not properly define their terminology.\r\rHolzer, Georg. 2007. Historische Grammatik des Kroatischen. Einleitung und Lautgeschichte\rder Standardsprache, Frankfurt am Main et al.: Peter Lang.\r\rWandl, Florian. 2020. On the Relative Chronology of the II Regressive and the Progressive\rPalatalizations of Common Slavic. Russian Linguistics 44.79\205108.\rhttps://doi.org/10.1007/s11185-020-09219-w) /CreationDate (D:20260826223418+02'00') /F 4 /M (D:20260914175728+02'00') /NM (f6e82898-f3a9-4a61-a440-c382d52a054f) /P 35 0 R /Popup 1122 0 R /QuadPoints [ 300.291 247.573 485.665 247.573 300.291 232.153 485.665 232.153 ] /RC (<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:26.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2" ><p dir="ltr"><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.6pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal">Here the issues start. There is no consensus about the relative chronology of these two palatalizations. In fact, the most elaborate reconstructions of the relative chronology of Slavic sound changes date the Progressive Palatalization \(= Third Palatalization\) before the monophthongization of diphthongs, e.g. Holzer \(2007\) \(= Pedersen's chronology\). The arguments for doing so have most recently been discussed by Wandl \(2020\). I also doubt that many scholars would call a phoneme system prior to these palatalization &quot;Late Common Slavic&quot;. Unfortunately, the authors do not properly define their terminology.&#13;&#13;</span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal">Holzer, Georg. 2007. </span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:italic">Historische Grammatik des Kroatischen</span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal">. Einleitung und Lautgeschichte&#13;der Standardsprache, Frankfurt am Main et al.: Peter Lang.&#13;&#13;Wandl, Florian. 2020. On the Relative Chronology of the II Regressive and the Progressive&#13;Palatalizations of Common Slavic. </span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:italic">Russian Linguistics </span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal">44.79&#8211;108.&#13;</span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal">https://doi.org/10.1007/s11185-020-09219-w</span></p></body>) /Rect [ 299.809 231.671 486.147 248.055 ] /Subj (Hervorheben) /Subtype /Highlight /T (Anonymous) /Type /Annot >>
endobj
