569 0 obj
<< /AP << /N 1276 0 R >> /C [ 0.588242 0.262741 0.988235 ] /CA 0.399994 /Contents (The use of the word "dejotation" seems unusual to me here. Normally the change of consonants + *j is referred to as jotation in Slavic, which includes the loss of the glide. I agree that it is probably more accurate to separate the loss of *j from the actual jotation change \(thus, e.g. Holzer 2007\), but then you should not speak of "dejotation reflexes" here. Or are the authors using the term "dejotation" as an equivalent to the usual term "jotation"?) /CreationDate (D:20260906175456+02'00') /F 4 /IT /HighlightNote /M (D:20260914180418+02'00') /NM (221f836d-5fb8-4ec6-b120-fc0f62ba28bd) /P 40 0 R /Popup 1277 0 R /QuadPoints [ 420.097 785.773 468.049 785.773 420.097 770.353 468.049 770.353 ] /RC (<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:26.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2" ><p dir="ltr"><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal">The use of the word &quot;dejotation&quot; seems unusual to me here. Normally the change of consonants + *j is referred to as jotation in Slavic, which includes the loss of the glide. I agree that it is probably more accurate to separate the loss of *j from the actual jotation change \(thus, e.g. Holzer 2007\), but then you should not speak of &quot;dejotation reflexes&quot; here. Or are the authors using the term &quot;dejotation&quot; as an equivalent to the usual term &quot;jotation&quot;?</span></p></body>) /Rect [ 419.615 769.871 468.531 786.255 ] /Subj (Kommentar zu Text) /Subtype /Highlight /T (Anonymous) /Type /Annot >>
endobj
570 0 obj
<< /F 28 /Open false /Parent 1278 0 R /Rect [ 595.304 693.773 779.304 785.773 ] /Subtype /Popup /Type /Annot >>
endobj
571 0 obj
<< /AP << /N 1279 0 R >> /C [ 0.588242 0.262741 0.988235 ] /CA 0.399994 /Contents (I don't understand why this should be the case. If the argument is that a change affecting the entire Slavic speech area could not have been preceded by changes affecting only parts of it, it is contradicted by many changes in the history of Slavic.) /CreationDate (D:20260906180359+02'00') /F 4 /IT /HighlightNote /M (D:20260914180426+02'00') /NM (56d412a8-2c87-41bc-8825-bb7fcb9fed01) /P 40 0 R /Popup 1280 0 R /QuadPoints [ 485.052 744.373 503.712 744.373 485.052 728.953 503.712 728.953 56.8 730.573 528.133 730.573 56.8 715.153 528.133 715.153 ] /RC (<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:26.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2" ><p dir="ltr"><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal">I don't understand why this should be the case. If the argument is that a change affecting the entire Slavic speech area could not have been preceded by changes affecting only parts of it, it is contradicted by many changes in the history of Slavic.</span></p></body>) /Rect [ 56.3181 714.671 528.615 744.855 ] /Subj (Kommentar zu Text) /Subtype /Highlight /T (Anonymous) /Type /Annot >>
endobj
572 0 obj
<< /F 28 /Open false /Parent 1281 0 R /Rect [ 595.304 652.373 779.304 744.373 ] /Subtype /Popup /Type /Annot >>
