<< /AP << /N 1683 0 R >> /C [ 0.588242 0.262741 0.988235 ] /CA 0.399994 /Contents (The use of the word "dejotation" seems unusual to me here. Normally the change of consonants + *j is referred to as jotation in Slavic, which includes the loss of the glide. I agree that it is probably more accurate to separate the loss of *j from the actual jotation change \(thus, e.g. Holzer 2007\), but then you should not speak of "dejotation reflexes" here. Or are the authors using the term "dejotation" as an equivalent to the usual term "jotation"?) /CreationDate (D:20260906175456+02'00') /F 4 /IT /HighlightNote /M (D:20260914180418+02'00') /NM (221f836d-5fb8-4ec6-b120-fc0f62ba28bd) /P 40 0 R /Popup 1277 0 R /QuadPoints [ 420.097 785.773 468.049 785.773 420.097 770.353 468.049 770.353 ] /RC (<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:26.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2" ><p dir="ltr"><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal">The use of the word &quot;dejotation&quot; seems unusual to me here. Normally the change of consonants + *j is referred to as jotation in Slavic, which includes the loss of the glide. I agree that it is probably more accurate to separate the loss of *j from the actual jotation change \(thus, e.g. Holzer 2007\), but then you should not speak of &quot;dejotation reflexes&quot; here. Or are the authors using the term &quot;dejotation&quot; as an equivalent to the usual term &quot;jotation&quot;?</span></p></body>) /Rect [ 419.615 769.871 468.531 786.255 ] /Subj (Kommentar zu Text) /Subtype /Highlight /T (Anonymous) /Type /Annot >>
endobj
