1403 0 obj
<< /AP << /N 1744 0 R >> /C [ 0.588242 0.262741 0.988235 ] /CA 0.399994 /Contents <> /CreationDate (D:20260906225433+02'00') /F 4 /IT /HighlightNote /M (D:20260914180751+02'00') /NM (59c120c5-adf6-45c5-bb19-f0ebe3d56e02) /P 48 0 R /Popup 1402 0 R /QuadPoints [ 323.658 103.359 436.878 103.359 323.658 90.5091 436.878 90.5091 ] /RC (<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:26.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2" ><p dir="ltr"><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal">But these forms are well known. Although this volume is dedicated to East Slavic, you can find the OCS endings discussed, for instance, in Kuznecov, et al. \(2006\). Recent contributions dealing with the rise of Slavic definite adjective endings are Sommer \(2019\) and especially Wandl \(2022\).&#13;&#13;</span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal">Kuznecov, Anatolii M., Sof&#8217;ja I. Iordanidi &amp; B. Krys&#8217;ko Vadim. 2006. Istori&#269;eskaja grammatika&#13;drevnerusskogo jazyka, vol. 3: Prilagatel&#8217;nye [Historical grammar of the Old Russian&#13;language, vol. 3: Adjectives]. Moskva: Azbukovnik.&#13;&#13;Sommer, Florian. 2019. Diachronie und areale Effekte. Zur Entstehung der bestimmten Adjektive&#13;im Baltischen und Slavischen. Die Sprache 52. 202&#8211;255.&#13;&#13;Wandl, Florian. &quot;Trapped morphology and the rise of the Slavic definite adjective inflection: a reexamination&quot; Folia Linguistica, vol. 56, no. s43-s1, 2022, pp. 1-31. https://doi.org/10.1515/flin-2022-2012</span></p></body>) /Rect [ 323.257 90.1075 437.279 103.761 ] /Subj (Kommentar zu Text) /Subtype /Highlight /T (Anonymous) /Type /Annot >>
endobj
